 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot K)$ where $N$ is LUT resolution and $K$ is the number of control points (for naive search) or $O(N \log K)$ with binary search [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the LUT storage [S1, book].
-*   **Performance:** Hardware interpolation is efficient, but frequent LUT updates (due to control point changes) can cause GPU bandwidth bottlenecks if not managed with dirty flags [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; supports smooth transitions via linear interpolation; compatible with standard GPU texture units [S1, book].
-*   **Limitations:** Linear interpolation may not capture complex non-linear mappings; 3D color cube interpolation causes visual artifacts [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** LUT generation is $O(N \cdot K)$ where $N$ is LUT size and $K$ is the number of control points (or $O(N \log K)$ if binary search is used) [S9, unofficial]. Sampling is $O(1)$ per voxel [S1, book].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the LUT storage [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Hardware texture sampling is extremely fast, making 1D TFs the standard for real-time volume rendering [S1, book].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, efficient, supports smooth interpolation [S1, book].
+*   **Limitations:** Cannot handle multivariate data (e.g., gradient magnitude) without extension to 2D TFs [S1, book]. Linear interpolation may not capture complex feature boundaries as well as higher-order methods [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originated as a standard technique in volume rendering to map scalar data to optical properties [S1, book]. It was widely adopted in early GPU-based volume rendering pipelines, such as those described in the NVIDIA GPU Gems series [S1, book]. Variants like 3D color cube interpolation were used in tools like Smokeview but were later superseded by 1D texture-based index interpolation to preserve colorbar integrity [S3, unofficial].
+The 1D transfer function is the simplest and most widely used classification method in volume rendering, introduced in early ray casting techniques [S1, book]. It maps scalar data values to optical properties, typically implemented using 1D texture lookup tables [S1, book]. The method evolved to use hardware-accelerated 1D textures to ensure interpolated colors remain within the defined colorbar, avoiding artifacts associated with 3D color cube interpolation [S3, unofficial].
 
